      "id": 1,
      "text": "Ponderosa was officially opened as a subdivision in 1963 after the Kramer family subdivided their land.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ponderosa%2C_California",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name comes from the abundant Ponderosa pine trees that once covered the area, a tree that also gave the subdivision its name.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://ponderosaca.com/about/history/",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Before becoming a community, the land served as a sheep pasture owned by Alex Kramer, who moved his flock to the mountains in 1925.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ponderosa%2C_California",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 2020, the Sequoia Complex Fire spared Ponderosa while nearby towns burned, preserving its historic character.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ponderosa%2C_California",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Kramer family has owned the property for three generations, with the original homestead founded in 1874 by Kramer who arrived from Pennsylvania.",
